# Chunking strategy

## Approach

Each downloaded document (`content.storage_path`) is split into overlapping
chunks using `langchain-text-splitters`' `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter`.
Only the chunk's position in the source text is stored — `start_offset` /
`end_offset` into the markdown file already in Storage — not the chunk text
itself (see `sql/0007_chunks.sql`). The actual text is sliced out on demand
when a chunk is selected for the LLM, avoiding duplicating the whole corpus
a second time in Postgres.

## Size

**1000 characters (~250 tokens) per chunk, 100 character (~10%) overlap.**

This is a middle ground between two competing pressures:

- Too large, and embeddings get diluted — a chunk covering several unrelated
  topics produces a vector that's a blurry average of all of them, weakening
  retrieval precision.
- Too small, and the LLM doesn't get enough context to judge materiality or
  valence — a bare sentence like "...in AI R&D" is uninterpretable without
  the subject and numbers that were in the previous sentence. Since the
  entire matched chunk (not a wider window) is what gets sent to the LLM for
  scoring, the chunk itself needs to be roughly paragraph-sized to be
  self-contained.

## Why RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter over a naive fixed-width split

A naive approach (slice every N characters, regardless of content) cuts
mid-word and mid-sentence constantly. The practical fix most naive
implementations reach for is *more overlap* — pad the boundary with enough
duplicated text that whatever got cut in half in one chunk is likely intact
in the next one. That works, but it's a blunt instrument: it inflates every
chunk's size (and therefore total storage and embedding cost) to compensate
for bad boundaries, rather than fixing the boundaries themselves.

`RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter` instead tries a prioritized list of natural
split points — paragraph breaks first, then sentence breaks, then word
breaks — only falling back to a hard character cut if nothing better is
available within the chunk size. In practice this means chunks almost always
start and end on a real sentence or paragraph boundary. The result: a much
smaller overlap (10%, vs. what a naive splitter would need to reliably avoid
losing meaning at boundaries) still gives clean, self-contained chunks —
less redundant text stored and embedded, for better chunk quality, not a
tradeoff between the two.

Offsets are returned natively (`add_start_index=True`), verified to slice
back to the exact original chunk text — no need to re-locate chunks in the
source document after the fact.
